twice each year the Setting Sun  perfectly aligned with the street grid  of Manhattan something called Manhattan  edge  here's why as the Earth rotates daily on  its axis an observer like me sweeps out  a circular trajectory and it is this  trajectory that defines what we mean by  east and west and also north and south  now if the Earth's rotation axis pointed  straight up and down then the Sun would  always rise due east and set due west  just as many of us were taught in school  but the rotation axis is actually tilted  by about 23 degrees and when it comes to  Manhattanhenge this is key in late June  the northern hemisphere is tilted toward  the Sun and this downward tilt makes the  sun's position when it rises and sets  shift a little northward six months  later in late December the northern  hemisphere points away from the Sun and  this upward tilt makes the sun's  position when it rises and sets shift a  little southward  and so in June I see the sunset north of  due west and in December I see the  sunset south of due west in between from  December to June the Sun sets a little  further north each evening and from June  to December  it sets a little further south each  evening this results in two special days  when the Sun set perfectly aligns with  the cross streets of Manhattan  Manhattanhenge and because of the  particular orientation of the New York  Street grid this occurs in late May and  mid-july near Memorial Day and  baseball's all-star game which inspired  my friend Neil Tyson to quip that our  distant descendants excavating in  ancient Manhattan will conclude that we  chose the grid orientation to honor war  and sports perhaps but I think our  descendants would more likely realize  that any other grid orientation would  have made a drive along Fifth Avenue a  somewhat less effective route for  traversing the city bottom line forget  symbolism just look and marvel  you
 

